<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Cupertino sends Apple Events, and under the hardened runtime that has to
       be declared. Without this key macOS does not merely refuse the event: it
       refuses to ASK. Measured on a reset grant, target running —

           com.apple.reminders running=true before=-1744 corePrompt=-1743

       errAEEventNotPermitted with no consent dialog, for any event class,
       because an app that never said it sends Apple Events is not worth
       prompting about.

       The MCP servers were unaffected and hid this for a long time: they reach
       Apple Events through `osascript`, a separate Apple-signed process that
       carries its own entitlement, with Cupertino only the responsible process
       TCC attributes the grant to. The one place the app sends an event in its
       own right is the Allow… button, and that is the one place this broke. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- The microphone, and the same shape of failure as the key above: the app
       is unsandboxed, but the HARDENED RUNTIME gates resource access too, and
       without this key macOS does not refuse the recording — it refuses to
       ASK.

       MEASURED, macOS 26.6, two Developer ID bundles signed with the
       hardened runtime, identical but for this one key:

           bare   before=notDetermined  requestAccess=false  after=denied
           audio  before=notDetermined  requestAccess=true   after=authorized

       `AVCaptureDevice.requestAccess` returns false immediately, with NO
       consent dialog, and leaves the status `denied`. That is a dead end and
       not a denial anyone can undo: the refusal writes NO TCC row, so the
       second run reads `notDetermined` all over again and Cupertino never
       appears in Privacy & Security › Microphone — which, unlike Full Disk
       Access and Screen Recording, has no "+" button to add it with. The
       "Allow…" button opened a pane where there was nothing to switch on.

       Also measured: re-signing WITH the key fixes it on the next launch, with
       no `tccutil reset` — there is no stale row to clear, which is the one
       good consequence of the refusal never having been recorded.

       scripts/spike-app-tcc missed this because build.sh signed its bundle
       without the hardened runtime; lane 2d measured a grant the shipping app
       could never reach.

       The codesign flag that turns it on cannot be named literally in here: a
       double hyphen is illegal inside an XML comment, and AMFI's parser is the
       strict one. `plutil -lint` accepts such a file happily, so the only
       symptom is `Failed to parse entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax
       error` from codesign — which the release job is the first thing to run,
       because a build with signing disabled never parses this file at all. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
